    [
     3,
     "\r<br>On Thu, Jan 19, 2017 at 1:26 PM, MEC &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">tlalli...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:\r<br>&gt; Community and GGPLOT2&#39;s Fans:\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; Sorry for this very basic question, I am new to R and new to ggplot2\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; I have the following toy code to use an example of what I am trying to\r<br>&gt; achieve:\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; library (&quot;lattice&quot;)\r<br>&gt; library (&quot;latticeExtra&quot;)\r<br>&gt; data (iris)\r<br>&gt; xyplot(Sepal.Width ~ Sepal.Length | Species, data = iris, panel =\r<br>&gt; function(x, y, ...) {\r<br>&gt;         panel.xyplot(x, y, ...)\r<br>&gt;         panel.lmlineq(x, y, adj = c(1,0),\r<br>&gt;             lty = 1,col.text=&#39;red&#39;, pos= 4,\r<br>&gt;             col.line = &quot;blue&quot;, digits = 1,r.squared =TRUE)\r<br>&gt;         panel.text(7, 4, round(cor(x, y),3), font=2, adj=c(0.5,-0.6))\r<br>&gt;         panel.text(7, 4, round(cor.test(x,y)$p.value, 3), font=1,\r<br>&gt; adj=c(0.5,0.6))},\r<br>&gt; xlab = &quot;Sepal.Length&quot;, ylab = &quot;Sepal_Width&quot;)\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; This codes generates an image (that I can&#39;t post), but you&#39;ll be able to see\r<br>&gt; if you use the code.\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; So, as you can see, I have a df with levels (Species) that I&#39;d like to plot\r<br>&gt; (all at the same time) showing their regression line with the Rsquared and\r<br>&gt; also printing their cor and cor.test outputs. Preferably, in an\r<br>&gt; aesthetically pleasing manner.\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; Has anyone tried to do something similiar? Is there an efficient way to do\r<br>&gt; it?\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; Originally @stackoverflow\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; Many thanks, any help will be much appreciated\r<br>&gt;\r<br>"
